      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Fix $t\\in[0,1]$. Since $\\sigma_t(x_1)>0$, solving $x=\\sigma_t(x_1)z+\\mu_t(x_1)$ gives the unique inverse $z=(x-\\mu_t(x_1))/\\sigma_t(x_1)$. Both the map and inverse are continuously differentiable in $z$, so $\\psi_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ is a diffeomorphism.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For fixed $z$, differentiation gives $\\frac{d}{dt}\\psi_t(z\\mid x_1)=\\dot\\sigma_t(x_1)z+\\dot\\mu_t(x_1)$. Substitute the inverse expression from step 1.1 to obtain $$\\frac{d}{dt}\\psi_t(z\\mid x_1)=\\frac{\\dot\\sigma_t(x_1)}{\\sigma_t(x_1)}\\bigl(\\psi_t(z\\mid x_1)-\\mu_t(x_1)\\bigr)+\\dot\\mu_t(x_1).$$ Thus the displayed $u_t$ satisfies the flow equation.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "If another field $\\widetilde u_t$ satisfies the same equation for every $z$, then for any $x$ choose the explicit inverse value $z=\\psi_t^{-1}(x\\mid x_1)$. The flow equation forces $\\widetilde u_t(x\\mid x_1)=\\frac{\\dot\\sigma_t(x_1)}{\\sigma_t(x_1)}(x-\\mu_t(x_1))+\\dot\\mu_t(x_1)$, proving uniqueness.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
