        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X,Y\\in L^2(\\mathbb P)$ are real random variables, then\n$$\\mathbb E[|XY|]\\le\\big(\\mathbb E[X^2]\\big)^{1/2}\\big(\\mathbb E[Y^2]\\big)^{1/2}.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $X,Y$ is zero almost surely, or\nthere is a constant $c>0$ with\n$$|X|^2=c\\,|Y|^2\\qquad\\mathbb P\\text{-almost surely}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space.\n\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is measurable, its **expectation** is the\n  extended-valued integral\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ or $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ is integrable in the sense\n  of Integrable real and complex functions, and their integrals, its\n  **expectation** is again\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P,$$\n  now a finite real or complex number.\n\nFor an integrable real random variable,\n$$\\mathbb E[X]=\\mathbb E[X^+]-\\mathbb E[X^-],$$\nwhere $X^+$ and $X^-$ are its positive and negative parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
