          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The affine maps $T(x)=a+sx$ and $x\\mapsto(x,T(x))$ are continuous, so their Borel preimages are Borel by [F5]; their laws are probability measures by [F2, F4]. Let $(X,Y)$ have joint law $\\gamma$. The first marginal gives $\\mathbb E_\\gamma X=0$ and $\\mathbb E_\\gamma\\|X\\|_2^2=m_2$. The second marginal is the law of $a+sX$ under $\\mu$, so change of variables [F3] and linearity [F6] give $\\mathbb E_\\gamma(Y-a)=0$ and $\\mathbb E_\\gamma\\|Y-a\\|_2^2=s^2m_2$. These second moments make all displayed costs finite; Cauchy–Schwarz [F8, F9] makes the cross term integrable.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Expand $Y-X=a+(Y-a)-X$. The expectation of the cross term with $a$ vanishes because $\\mathbb E_\\gamma(Y-a)=\\mathbb E_\\gamma X=0$, so $$\\mathbb E_\\gamma\\|Y-X\\|_2^2=\\|a\\|_2^2+(1+s^2)m_2-2\\mathbb E_\\gamma\\langle X,Y-a\\rangle.$$ By [F8], pointwise $|\\langle X,Y-a\\rangle|\\le\\|X\\|_2\\|Y-a\\|_2$. Applying [F9] to the two scalar norms gives $\\mathbb E_\\gamma\\langle X,Y-a\\rangle\\le\\sqrt{m_2\\,s^2m_2}=s m_2$. Substitution proves the lower bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The law of $(X,T(X))$ for $X\\sim\\mu$ is a coupling with marginals $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ by [F2, F4, F5]. Under this coupling $Y-a=sX$, so $\\mathbb E\\langle X,Y-a\\rangle=sm_2$. The expansion in step 2.1 therefore gives cost $\\|a\\|_2^2+(1-s)^2m_2$, attaining the lower bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F10",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_smoke": [
        {
          "check": "ml101-flow-unit-scale-boundary",
          "asserts": "Let $\\mu$ be a Borel probability measure on $\\mathbb R^d$ with finite second moment, mean $\\int x\\,\\mu(dx)=0$, and $m_2:=\\int\\|x\\|_2^2\\,\\mu(dx)<\\infty$. Fix $a\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $s>0$, define $T(x):=a+sx$, and let $\\nu$ be the law of $T(X)$ when $X$ has law $\\mu$. For every Borel probability measure $\\gamma$ on $\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d$ whose coordinate marginals are $\\mu$ and $\\nu$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d}\\|y-x\\|_2^2\\,\\gamma(dx,dy)\\ \\ge\\ \\|a\\|_2^2+(1-s)^2m_2.$$\nThe law of $(X,T(X))$ under $\\mu$ attains equality. Thus $T$ is an optimal transport map for the squared Euclidean cost between $\\mu$ and its translated dilation $\\nu$.",
