    "thm-conditional-flow-matching-gradient-identity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-probability-path-and-velocity-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$, a sigma-finite measure $\\lambda$ on the Borel subsets of $\\mathbb R^d$, and a Borel probability measure $\\pi$ on $\\mathbb R^d$. Here sigma-finite means that there are Borel sets $E_n$ with $\\mathbb R^d=\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}E_n$ and $\\lambda(E_n)<\\infty$ for each $n$. A **conditional probability-density path** with endpoint law $\\pi$ is a function measurable for $\\mathcal B([0,1])\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)$ on time, state, and endpoint variables\n$$\\rho:[0,1]\\times\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty]$$\nsuch that, for every $t\\in[0,1]$, $\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\lambda(dx)=1$ for $\\pi$-almost every endpoint $x_1$. Its **mixture function** is\n$$p_t(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1).$$\nFor a vector field $u_t(x\\mid x_1)=(u_t^1,\\ldots,u_t^d)$ measurable for the same product sigma-algebra, call it a **conditional velocity field** for $\\rho$ when, for $\\pi$-almost every $x_1$, $\\rho_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ and every component of $\\rho_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)u_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ are continuously differentiable in $(t,x)$ on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$, the functions $\\rho_t$ and $\\rho_tu_t^j$ are locally integrable there with respect to $dt\\,\\lambda(dx)$, and\n$$\\int_0^1\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\left(\\partial_t\\varphi(t,x)+\\sum_{j=1}^d u_t^j(x\\mid x_1)\\partial_{x_j}\\varphi(t,x)\\right)\\lambda(dx)\\,dt=0$$\nfor every continuously differentiable real function $\\varphi$ with compact support in $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$. Here $dt$ denotes the supplied Lebesgue measure on time, and local integrability means absolute integrability on every compact subset. This is the **conditional continuity equation** for the measures $\\rho_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)\\lambda$. In the Lebesgue-reference case $\\lambda(dx)=dx$, its classical form under the stated differentiability is\n$$\\partial_t\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)+\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}\\!\\left(\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)u_t^j(x\\mid x_1)\\right)=0$$\non $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$. This unweighted divergence formula is not the convention for a general reference measure. Only the interior-time equation is imposed; no endpoint continuity or existence or uniqueness of an ODE flow is asserted. At any $(t,x)$ where $0<p_t(x)<\\infty$ and the numerator is a finite componentwise Lebesgue integral, the **marginal velocity field** is\n$$u_t(x):=\\frac{\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)u_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1)}{p_t(x)}.$$\nThis definition assigns no value to the quotient where $p_t(x)=0$ or $p_t(x)=\\infty$, or where the numerator is not finite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-conditional-mixture-is-a-probability-path",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\lambda$, $\\pi$, and $\\rho$ satisfy the conditional probability-density path definition, and for each $t\\in[0,1]$ set $p_t(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1)$. Then $p_t$ is a nonnegative measurable function and $\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}p_t(x)\\,\\lambda(dx)=1$ for every $t$; hence it is a probability density with respect to $\\lambda$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-conditional-to-marginal-continuity-equation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\lambda$, $\\pi$, and $\\rho$ be as in the conditional probability-path definition, and let $u$ be a jointly measurable vector field. Suppose that for $\\pi$-almost every $x_1$, $\\rho$ and each component of $\\rho u$ are continuously differentiable in $(t,x)$ and satisfy the classical coordinate equation $\\partial_t\\rho_t+\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}(\\rho_tu_t^j)=0$ on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$. This is an explicit algebraic hypothesis; for general $\\lambda$ it is not the definition of measure transport. Suppose also that for every compact $K\\subset(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$ there is a nonnegative $G_K\\in L^1(\\pi)$ which, for $\\pi$-almost every $x_1$, bounds on $K$ the absolute values of $\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)$, $\\partial_t\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)$, each component of $\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)u_t(x\\mid x_1)$, and each derivative $\\partial_{x_j}(\\rho_tu_t^j)(x\\mid x_1)$. If $p_t(x)>0$ on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$, define\n$$J_t(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)u_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1), \\qquad u_t(x):=\\frac{J_t(x)}{p_t(x)}.$$\nThen $\\partial_t p_t$ and each indicated flux derivative $\\partial_{x_j}J_t^j$ exist and are continuous locally in $(t,x)$, and the marginal continuity equation\n$$\\partial_t p_t(x)+\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}\\!\\left(p_t(x)u_t^j(x)\\right)=0$$\nholds on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$. The domination hypothesis is a sufficient Leibniz condition for the time and spatial derivatives to pass through the $x_1$ integral.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ (The natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$ (von Neumann),\nOn $\\mathbb{N}$ the order is membership: $m < n \\iff m \\in n$), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of The vector space $F^{X}$ of all functions $X \\to F$ with pointwise operations, and $F^{n}$ as the case $X = n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n(Vector space over a field). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of Finite sums and finite products, by recursion applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 4 and\nSquares of nonzero elements are positive, the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\nInteger powers $a^m$) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot (Square roots exist: a unique $\\sqrt{a} \\ge 0$ with $(\\sqrt{a})^2 = a$; the positives are $\\{x^2 : x \\neq 0\\}$).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space (The standard list $e : n \\to F^{n}$ with $e_i(i) = 1_F$ and $e_i(j) = 0_F$ for $j \\ne i$ is an ordered basis of $F^{n}$; hence $\\dim_F F^{n} = n$, and $F^{0}$ is the zero space with basis $\\varnothing$ and dimension $0$ clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and Conventions of this page, the standing $n \\ge 1$ hypothesis, and what is taken up elsewhere in the reading order lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ (Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   (Squares of nonzero elements are positive), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ($\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of A norm on a real vector space, the induced metric, and the dictionary with the metric axioms\nis proved in Cauchy-Schwarz $\\lvert\\langle x,y\\rangle\\rvert \\le \\lVert x\\rVert_2\\lVert y\\rVert_2$ with its equality case, the triangle inequality for $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$, the parallelogram law and polarisation, where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ (Continuity of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point of $A$ and on $A$: the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ condition, its agreement with $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)$ at a limit point, and continuity at an isolated point, Intervals of $\\mathbb{R}$: the nine order-convex forms, nondegeneracy, and length) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n(The derivative $f'(c) = \\lim_{x \\to c} \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c}$ of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point $c \\in A$ that is a limit point of $A$, and differentiability on a set). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary measure space. All functions below have domain $X$. Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The class $L^1(\\mu)$ is a complex vector space, and the Lebesgue integral is\ncomplex-linear on it:\n$$\\int (\\alpha f+\\beta g)\\,d\\mu=\\alpha\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\beta\\int g\\,d\\mu \\qquad(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C,\\ f,g\\in L^1(\\mu)).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and let $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, with the Euclidean\ninner product and the Euclidean norm as in The Euclidean inner product $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k y_k$ on $\\mathbb{R}^n$. Then:\n\n1. **Cauchy-Schwarz.**\n   $$\\lvert\\langle x,y\\rangle\\rvert \\;\\le\\; \\lVert x\\rVert_2\\,\\lVert y\\rVert_2 ,$$\n   with equality if and only if there is a pair $(\\lambda,\\mu) \\ne (0,0)$ of\n   reals with $\\lambda x_k = \\mu y_k$ for every $k < n$.\n2. **$\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$**\n   (A norm on a real vector space, the induced metric, and the dictionary with the metric axioms), for **every** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$; the metric\n   it induces is $d_2$ of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it whenever $n \\ge 1$.\n3. **Parallelogram law.**\n   $$\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} + \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2} \\;=\\; 2\\lVert x\\rVert_2^{2} + 2\\lVert y\\rVert_2^{2} .$$\n4. **Polarisation.**\n   $$\\langle x,y\\rangle \\;=\\; \\tfrac14\\Bigl(\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} - \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2}\\Bigr),$$\n   so the inner product is recovered from the norm it induces.\n\n**Clause 1 is a citation, not a new proof.** The inequality and its equality case\nare the published The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for finite sums, stated there for two lists of\nreals; all that happens below is that it is read in the vector notation of\nThe Euclidean inner product $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k y_k$ on $\\mathbb{R}^n$. Re-proving it here would put two proofs of one\nstatement in the library.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X,Y\\in L^2(\\mathbb P)$ are real random variables, then\n$$\\mathbb E[|XY|]\\le\\big(\\mathbb E[X^2]\\big)^{1/2}\\big(\\mathbb E[Y^2]\\big)^{1/2}.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $X,Y$ is zero almost surely, or\nthere is a constant $c>0$ with\n$$|X|^2=c\\,|Y|^2\\qquad\\mathbb P\\text{-almost surely}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1] and the mixture normalization result [F2], each $p_t$ is a probability density with respect to $\\lambda$. Positivity makes $u^m=J/p$ well-defined on the open time interval. The hypotheses on $G_K$ are exactly the local domination assumptions of [F3], so the explicitly assumed classical coordinate equation passes to the marginal and $\\partial_tp+\\operatorname{div}_x(pu^m)=0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $Q$ be the measure on time, state, and endpoint variables with density $\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)$ relative to $dt\\,\\lambda(dx)\\,\\pi(dx_1)$. Product measurability from [F1], conditional normalization, and Tonelli [F14] show that $Q$ has total mass one and that its time-state marginal has density $p_t(x)$ relative to $dt\\,\\lambda(dx)$. In particular, Tonelli gives $\\mathcal L_M(\\theta)=\\int\\|v-u^m\\|_2^2\\,dQ$, so the finite marginal loss makes $v-u^m$ square-integrable under $Q$. The same identity gives $C=\\int\\|u^c-u^m\\|_2^2\\,dQ$, so $u^c-u^m$ is square-integrable under $Q$. By Euclidean Cauchy–Schwarz [F9] and random-variable Cauchy–Schwarz [F10], their inner-product term is integrable. At every $t,x$ where the integrals are defined, the definition of $J_t$ and $u^m_t$ gives $$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\bigl(u^c_t(x\\mid x_1)-u^m_t(x)\\bigr)\\,\\pi(dx_1)=J_t(x)-p_t(x)u^m_t(x)=0.$$ Expanding $v-u^c=(v-u^m)+(u^m-u^c)$ by bilinearity [F4], integrating first over $x_1$, and using Fubini [F5] for the integrable cross term gives $$\\mathcal L_C(\\theta)=\\mathcal L_M(\\theta)+\\int_0^1\\!\\int\\!\\int\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\|u^c_t(x\\mid x_1)-u^m_t(x)\\|_2^2\\,\\pi(dx_1)\\lambda(dx)dt.$$ The last integral is independent of $\\theta$ and is the finite constant $C$ defined in the Statement.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F14",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "Fix $\\theta_0\\in\\Theta$ and choose a compact parameter ball $H\\subset\\Theta$ whose interior contains $\\theta_0$. For any $\\theta\\in\\operatorname{int}H$, fix $w$ to be either $u^c_t(x\\mid x_1)$ or $u^m_t(x)$ and put $a_\\theta:=v(t,x;\\theta)-w$. For sufficiently small nonzero $\\varepsilon$ with $\\theta+\\varepsilon e_j\\in H$, bilinearity and symmetry [F4] give $$\\|a_{\\theta+\\varepsilon e_j}\\|_2^2-\\|a_\\theta\\|_2^2=\\langle a_{\\theta+\\varepsilon e_j}-a_\\theta,a_{\\theta+\\varepsilon e_j}+a_\\theta\\rangle.$$ Dividing by $\\varepsilon$ and taking the limit, differentiability of $v$ yields $$\\partial_{\\theta_j}\\|v(t,x;\\theta)-w\\|_2^2=2\\langle\\partial_{\\theta_j}v(t,x;\\theta),v(t,x;\\theta)-w\\rangle.$$ The functions $G^C_{H,j}$ and $G^M_{H,j}$ dominate the weighted derivatives on $H$. For a sequence of nonzero increments tending to zero in coordinate $j$, the mean value theorem [F6] bounds the weighted difference quotients by the same majorants; the pointwise quotients converge to the displayed derivatives. Dominated convergence [F7] therefore differentiates both objectives under their integrals. Applying the same domination to convergent parameter sequences shows the derivative integrals are continuous in $\\theta$, so both objectives are $C^1$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Step 1.2 proves $\\mathcal L_C(\\theta)=\\mathcal L_M(\\theta)+C$ for every $\\theta$, with one finite constant independent of $\\theta$. Both sides are differentiable by step 1.3, so differentiating this identity gives $\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_C(\\theta)=\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_M(\\theta)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
