    "thm-gaussian-conditional-optimal-transport-map": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Define $\\gamma(E)=\\int_E e^{-x^2/2}/\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,dx$ for Borel E in $\\mathbb R$. By The standard normal density has total mass one and The indefinite integral of a nonnegative measurable function is a measure, gamma is a probability measure; denote it $N(0,1)$. For $m\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$, define $N(m,\\sigma^2)$ as the law of $x\\mapsto m+\\sigma x$ on $(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B,\\gamma)$. This affine map is continuous: for $\\sigma$>0 choose $\\delta$=$\\varepsilon$/$\\sigma$, and for $\\sigma$=0 it is constant. Its inverse images of opens are open, so it is Borel measurable. The law of a random element is a probability measure makes its pushforward a probability. When $\\sigma$=0, the preimage of E is all of R if m belongs to E and empty otherwise, so $N(m,0)=\\delta_m$ in The Dirac set function at a point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$ be finite. For $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and a real symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $\\Sigma$, a Borel probability law is denoted $N_d(m,\\Sigma)$ when a vector X with that law satisfies $u\\cdot X\\sim N(u\\cdot m,u^T\\Sigma u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Such a law exists, has mean m and covariance Sigma, and can be realized as $m+\\Sigma^{1/2}Z$ with independent standard-normal coordinates. Singular Sigma is allowed. Uniqueness will be proved in the following characteristic-function lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nondegenerate-multivariate-normal-law-has-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $d\\ge1$, $m\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let $\\Sigma$ be a real symmetric positive-definite $d\\times d$ matrix. If $X\\sim N_d(m,\\Sigma)$, then its law has Lebesgue density\n$$f_{m,\\Sigma}(x)=(2\\pi)^{-d/2}\\det(\\Sigma)^{-1/2} \\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-m)^T\\Sigma^{-1}(x-m)\\right), \\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-affine-conditional-path-velocity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, fix $x_1\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let $t\\mapsto\\mu_t(x_1)\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $t\\mapsto\\sigma_t(x_1)\\in(0,\\infty)$ be continuously differentiable on $[0,1]$, with one-sided derivatives at the endpoints. For $z\\in\\mathbb R^d$, define the affine map\n$$\\psi_t(z\\mid x_1):=\\sigma_t(x_1)z+\\mu_t(x_1).$$\nFor each $t$, this map is a diffeomorphism with inverse $\\psi_t^{-1}(x\\mid x_1)=(x-\\mu_t(x_1))/\\sigma_t(x_1)$. The unique vector field satisfying\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\psi_t(z\\mid x_1)=u_t(\\psi_t(z\\mid x_1)\\mid x_1)$$\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb R^d$ is\n$$u_t(x\\mid x_1)=\\frac{\\dot\\sigma_t(x_1)}{\\sigma_t(x_1)}(x-\\mu_t(x_1))+\\dot\\mu_t(x_1).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-affine-dilation-is-quadratic-cost-optimal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mu$ be a Borel probability measure on $\\mathbb R^d$ with finite second moment, mean $\\int x\\,\\mu(dx)=0$, and $m_2:=\\int\\|x\\|_2^2\\,\\mu(dx)<\\infty$. Fix $a\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $s>0$, define $T(x):=a+sx$, and let $\\nu$ be the law of $T(X)$ when $X$ has law $\\mu$. For every Borel probability measure $\\gamma$ on $\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d$ whose coordinate marginals are $\\mu$ and $\\nu$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d}\\|y-x\\|_2^2\\,\\gamma(dx,dy)\\ \\ge\\ \\|a\\|_2^2+(1-s)^2m_2.$$\nThe law of $(X,T(X))$ under $\\mu$ attains equality. Thus $T$ is an optimal transport map for the squared Euclidean cost between $\\mu$ and its translated dilation $\\nu$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ (The natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$ (von Neumann),\nOn $\\mathbb{N}$ the order is membership: $m < n \\iff m \\in n$), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of The vector space $F^{X}$ of all functions $X \\to F$ with pointwise operations, and $F^{n}$ as the case $X = n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n(Vector space over a field). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of Finite sums and finite products, by recursion applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 4 and\nSquares of nonzero elements are positive, the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\nInteger powers $a^m$) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot (Square roots exist: a unique $\\sqrt{a} \\ge 0$ with $(\\sqrt{a})^2 = a$; the positives are $\\{x^2 : x \\neq 0\\}$).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space (The standard list $e : n \\to F^{n}$ with $e_i(i) = 1_F$ and $e_i(j) = 0_F$ for $j \\ne i$ is an ordered basis of $F^{n}$; hence $\\dim_F F^{n} = n$, and $F^{0}$ is the zero space with basis $\\varnothing$ and dimension $0$ clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and Conventions of this page, the standing $n \\ge 1$ hypothesis, and what is taken up elsewhere in the reading order lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ (Laws of finite sums and finite products clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   (Squares of nonzero elements are positive), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ($\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of A norm on a real vector space, the induced metric, and the dictionary with the metric axioms\nis proved in Cauchy-Schwarz $\\lvert\\langle x,y\\rangle\\rvert \\le \\lVert x\\rVert_2\\lVert y\\rVert_2$ with its equality case, the triangle inequality for $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$, the parallelogram law and polarisation, where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n(Limit point, isolated point, adherent point, derived set, and dense subset of $\\mathbb{R}$), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n(The derivative $f'(c) = \\lim_{x \\to c} \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c}$ of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point $c \\in A$ that is a limit point of $A$, and differentiability on a set) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of If $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = L \\ne 0$ then $|f| > |L|/2$ on a punctured neighbourhood of $c$; in particular if $L > 0$ then $f > L/2 > 0$ there applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\nCarathéodory's characterisation: $f$ is differentiable at $c$ if and only if there is $\\varphi : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$, continuous at $c$, with $f(x) - f(c) = \\varphi(x)(x - c)$ for every $x \\in A$, and then $\\varphi$ is unique and $\\varphi(c) = f'(c)$.** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of Sums, scalar multiples, products, absolute values, maxima, minima and quotients with nonvanishing denominator of continuous functions are continuous, as are constants, the identity and every polynomial function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Since $\\sigma_t=(1-t)+t\\sigma_{\\min}>0$, every affine map $\\psi_t$ is invertible. For each $v\\in\\mathbb R^d$, the projection of $\\psi_t(Z\\mid x_1)$ is $v^T\\mu_t(x_1)+\\sigma_t v^TZ$. By [F1], this is normal with mean $v^T\\mu_t(x_1)$ and variance $\\sigma_t^2\\|v\\|_2^2$. Thus $P_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ is multivariate normal with mean $\\mu_t(x_1)$ and covariance $\\sigma_t^2I_d$, which is positive definite.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "By [F2], the Lebesgue density of $P_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ is $$\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)=(2\\pi\\sigma_t^2)^{-d/2} \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|x-\\mu_t(x_1)\\|_2^2}{2\\sigma_t^2}\\right).$$ It integrates to one for every $x_1$ and is continuous in $(t,x,x_1)$ because $\\sigma_t>0$. Therefore for any endpoint probability law $\\pi$ it is product-measurable and satisfies the conditional-density requirement in [[def-probability-path-and-velocity-field]].",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "We have $\\dot\\mu_t(x_1)=x_1$ and $\\dot\\sigma_t=\\sigma_{\\min}-1$. Applying [F3] gives $u_t(x\\mid x_1)=x_1+\\frac{\\sigma_{\\min}-1}{\\sigma_t}(x-tx_1)$. The density formula in step 1.2 and this affine field are continuously differentiable in $(t,x)$ by the algebra, chain, and exponential derivative rules [F6, F7, F8]. Thus $\\rho_t$ and each component of $\\rho_tu_t$ meet the regularity clauses of the conditional probability-path definition.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "At $t=0$ the parameters are $\\mu_0=0,\\sigma_0=1$; at $t=1$ they are $\\mu_1=x_1,\\sigma_1=\\sigma_{\\min}$. Thus the endpoints are the standard Gaussian law and the law of $x_1+\\sigma_{\\min}Z$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Put $r=x-tx_1$ and write $\\dot\\sigma=\\sigma_{\\min}-1$. We have $\\partial_t r=-x_1$ and $\\partial_t(\\sigma_t^{-d})=-d\\dot\\sigma\\sigma_t^{-d-1}$ by the product and quotient rules [F6]. Applying the chain rule [F7] to $-\\|r\\|_2^2/(2\\sigma_t^2)$ and then the exponential derivative [F8] gives $$\\partial_t\\rho_t=\\rho_t\\left(-d\\frac{\\dot\\sigma}{\\sigma_t} +\\frac{\\langle r,x_1\\rangle}{\\sigma_t^2} +\\frac{\\dot\\sigma\\|r\\|_2^2}{\\sigma_t^3}\\right), \\qquad \\nabla_x\\rho_t=-\\rho_t\\frac{r}{\\sigma_t^2}.$$ The field in step 2.1 has $\\operatorname{div}_x u_t=d\\dot\\sigma/\\sigma_t$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "The product rule gives $$\\operatorname{div}_x(\\rho_tu_t) =\\langle\\nabla_x\\rho_t,u_t\\rangle+\\rho_t\\operatorname{div}_xu_t =\\rho_t\\left(-\\frac{\\langle r,x_1\\rangle}{\\sigma_t^2} -\\frac{\\dot\\sigma\\|r\\|_2^2}{\\sigma_t^3} +d\\frac{\\dot\\sigma}{\\sigma_t}\\right).$$ Adding this to $\\partial_t\\rho_t$ in step 3.1 cancels each term, so the conditional continuity equation holds.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "The standard Gaussian vector has mean $0$ and covariance $I_d$ by [F1]. Summing its $d$ coordinate second moments gives $\\int\\|z\\|_2^2\\,N_d(0,I_d)(dz)=d$. The endpoint law is the affine image under $T(z)=x_1+\\sigma_{\\min}z$. Applying [F4] with $a=x_1$, $s=\\sigma_{\\min}$, and $m_2=d$ shows that every coupling has cost at least $\\|x_1\\|_2^2+d(1-\\sigma_{\\min})^2$, and that the law of $(Z,T(Z))$ attains equality.",
          "inputs": [
