    "ex-mixture-of-straight-paths-has-nonlinear-marginal-velocity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-gaussian-conditional-optimal-transport-map",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $d\\ge1$, fix $x_1\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and choose $\\sigma_{\\min}>0$. Let $Z$ have the standard multivariate normal law $N_d(0,I_d)$. For $t\\in[0,1]$, define\n$$\\mu_t(x_1):=t x_1,\\qquad \\sigma_t:=1-(1-\\sigma_{\\min})t,\\qquad \\psi_t(z\\mid x_1):=\\sigma_t z+\\mu_t(x_1),$$\nand let $P_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ be the law of $\\psi_t(Z\\mid x_1)$. Then $P_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ is the Gaussian probability path with mean $\\mu_t(x_1)$ and covariance $\\sigma_t^2I_d$, and its Lebesgue density is\n$$\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)=(2\\pi\\sigma_t^2)^{-d/2} \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|x-\\mu_t(x_1)\\|_2^2}{2\\sigma_t^2}\\right).$$\nA conditional velocity field for this path is\n$$u_t(x\\mid x_1)=\\frac{\\sigma_{\\min}-1}{\\sigma_t}(x-tx_1)+x_1.$$\nFor each endpoint $x_1$, these densities and fields form a conditional path in the probability-path definition; hence they can be mixed against any Borel endpoint law. At $t=0$ the law is $N_d(0,I_d)$, and at $t=1$ it is the law of $x_1+\\sigma_{\\min}Z$, a Gaussian with mean $x_1$ and covariance $\\sigma_{\\min}^2I_d$. The affine endpoint map $T(z)=x_1+\\sigma_{\\min}z$ is optimal for squared Euclidean cost between these two laws: every coupling has cost at least\n$$\\|x_1\\|_2^2+d(1-\\sigma_{\\min})^2,$$\nand the law of $(Z,T(Z))$ attains this value.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-probability-path-and-velocity-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$, a sigma-finite measure $\\lambda$ on the Borel subsets of $\\mathbb R^d$, and a Borel probability measure $\\pi$ on $\\mathbb R^d$. Here sigma-finite means that there are Borel sets $E_n$ with $\\mathbb R^d=\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}E_n$ and $\\lambda(E_n)<\\infty$ for each $n$. A **conditional probability-density path** with endpoint law $\\pi$ is a function measurable for $\\mathcal B([0,1])\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)\\otimes\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)$ on time, state, and endpoint variables\n$$\\rho:[0,1]\\times\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty]$$\nsuch that, for every $t\\in[0,1]$, $\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\lambda(dx)=1$ for $\\pi$-almost every endpoint $x_1$. Its **mixture function** is\n$$p_t(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1).$$\nFor a vector field $u_t(x\\mid x_1)=(u_t^1,\\ldots,u_t^d)$ measurable for the same product sigma-algebra, call it a **conditional velocity field** for $\\rho$ when, for $\\pi$-almost every $x_1$, $\\rho_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ and every component of $\\rho_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)u_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)$ are continuously differentiable in $(t,x)$ on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$, the functions $\\rho_t$ and $\\rho_tu_t^j$ are locally integrable there with respect to $dt\\,\\lambda(dx)$, and\n$$\\int_0^1\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\left(\\partial_t\\varphi(t,x)+\\sum_{j=1}^d u_t^j(x\\mid x_1)\\partial_{x_j}\\varphi(t,x)\\right)\\lambda(dx)\\,dt=0$$\nfor every continuously differentiable real function $\\varphi$ with compact support in $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$. Here $dt$ denotes the supplied Lebesgue measure on time, and local integrability means absolute integrability on every compact subset. This is the **conditional continuity equation** for the measures $\\rho_t(\\cdot\\mid x_1)\\lambda$. In the Lebesgue-reference case $\\lambda(dx)=dx$, its classical form under the stated differentiability is\n$$\\partial_t\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)+\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}\\!\\left(\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)u_t^j(x\\mid x_1)\\right)=0$$\non $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$. This unweighted divergence formula is not the convention for a general reference measure. Only the interior-time equation is imposed; no endpoint continuity or existence or uniqueness of an ODE flow is asserted. At any $(t,x)$ where $0<p_t(x)<\\infty$ and the numerator is a finite componentwise Lebesgue integral, the **marginal velocity field** is\n$$u_t(x):=\\frac{\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)u_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1)}{p_t(x)}.$$\nThis definition assigns no value to the quotient where $p_t(x)=0$ or $p_t(x)=\\infty$, or where the numerator is not finite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-conditional-mixture-is-a-probability-path",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\lambda$, $\\pi$, and $\\rho$ satisfy the conditional probability-density path definition, and for each $t\\in[0,1]$ set $p_t(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1)$. Then $p_t$ is a nonnegative measurable function and $\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}p_t(x)\\,\\lambda(dx)=1$ for every $t$; hence it is a probability density with respect to $\\lambda$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space (Open cover, subcover, compact metric space, and compact subset of a metric space,\nMetric space: $d(x,y) = 0$ iff $x = y$, symmetry, and the triangle inequality; pseudometric and ultrametric) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n(Continuity of a map between metric spaces, at a point and globally, in the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ form), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ (The absolute value makes $\\mathbb{R}$ a metric space: $d(x,y) = |x-y|$ is a metric, its open balls are the intervals $(x-r, x+r)$, and it is unbounded). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below (Lower bound, bounded below, bounded set), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum (Maximum and minimum of a set): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n(Complete ordered field (least-upper-bound property), Greatest lower bound (infimum)).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-conditional-to-marginal-continuity-equation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\lambda$, $\\pi$, and $\\rho$ be as in the conditional probability-path definition, and let $u$ be a jointly measurable vector field. Suppose that for $\\pi$-almost every $x_1$, $\\rho$ and each component of $\\rho u$ are continuously differentiable in $(t,x)$ and satisfy the classical coordinate equation $\\partial_t\\rho_t+\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}(\\rho_tu_t^j)=0$ on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$. This is an explicit algebraic hypothesis; for general $\\lambda$ it is not the definition of measure transport. Suppose also that for every compact $K\\subset(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$ there is a nonnegative $G_K\\in L^1(\\pi)$ which, for $\\pi$-almost every $x_1$, bounds on $K$ the absolute values of $\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)$, $\\partial_t\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)$, each component of $\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)u_t(x\\mid x_1)$, and each derivative $\\partial_{x_j}(\\rho_tu_t^j)(x\\mid x_1)$. If $p_t(x)>0$ on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$, define\n$$J_t(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)u_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1), \\qquad u_t(x):=\\frac{J_t(x)}{p_t(x)}.$$\nThen $\\partial_t p_t$ and each indicated flux derivative $\\partial_{x_j}J_t^j$ exist and are continuous locally in $(t,x)$, and the marginal continuity equation\n$$\\partial_t p_t(x)+\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_{x_j}\\!\\left(p_t(x)u_t^j(x)\\right)=0$$\nholds on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R^d$. The domination hypothesis is a sufficient Leibniz condition for the time and spatial derivatives to pass through the $x_1$ integral.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in The exponential series converges absolutely for every real argument. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of The factorial $n!$ and the falling factorial $n^{\\underline{k}}$, defined by recursion in $\\mathbb{N}$, $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image (The canonical natural $\\iota(n) = n \\cdot 1_F$ of a field, Canonical naturals are positive and strictly increasing), and powers and series are those of Integer powers $a^m$ and Series, partial sums, convergence and the sum, divergence, and the tail series.\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ (A real power series about a centre, its interval of convergence, and its radius in $[0,+\\infty]$). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-exponential-addition-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all real $x,y$,\n$$\\exp(x+y)=\\exp(x)\\exp(y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The exponential function is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$, $\\exp(x)>0$ and\n$$\\exp(-x)=\\frac1{\\exp(x)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
