          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1] to the two endpoints $x_1=a$ and $x_1=-a$. Their conditional densities are positive and continuous in $(t,x)$, as are their time derivatives, fluxes, and spatial flux derivatives; $\\sigma_t>0$ throughout $[0,1]$. Hence for each compact $K\\subset(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R$ the finitely many such functions are bounded on $K$ by [F4]. Taking a common bound as the constant function $G_K$ gives an element of $L^1(\\pi)$ because $\\pi$ has total mass one. Thus [F5] applies. The mixture density is normalized by [F3] and is strictly positive because it is a sum of two positive Gaussian densities.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Put $$A_+(t,x):=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{(x-ta)^2}{2\\sigma_t^2}\\right),\\qquad A_-(t,x):=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{(x+ta)^2}{2\\sigma_t^2}\\right).$$ The common Gaussian normalizing factor cancels in the endpoint-weighted mean, so [F2] gives $$m_t(x):=\\frac{\\int x_1\\rho_t(x\\mid x_1)\\,\\pi(dx_1)}{p_t(x)} =a\\frac{A_+(t,x)-A_-(t,x)}{A_+(t,x)+A_-(t,x)}.$$ Expanding the two squares gives $A_+/A_-=\\exp(2atx/\\sigma_t^2)$ by [F7] and [F9]. Multiplying numerator and denominator by $\\exp(-atx/\\sigma_t^2)$ therefore yields $m_t(x)=a\\tanh(atx/\\sigma_t^2)$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For endpoint $x_1\\in\\{-a,a\\}$, [F1] gives $$u^c_t(x\\mid x_1)=cx+(1-tc)x_1, \\qquad c:=\\frac{\\sigma_{\\min}-1}{\\sigma_t}.$$ Taking the endpoint-weighted mean and using step 1.2 gives $u^m_t(x)=cx+(1-tc)m_t(x)$. Since $\\sigma_t=1+t(\\sigma_{\\min}-1)$, we have $\\sigma_t-t(\\sigma_{\\min}-1)=1$ and hence $1-tc=1/\\sigma_t$. Substitution gives the marginal velocity formula in the Example.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix $t\\in(0,1)$ and put $\\beta:=a/\\sigma_t>0$ and $k:=at/\\sigma_t^2>0$. The formula for $\\tanh$, the addition formula [F7], and the reciprocal identity [F9] give $\\tanh(-y)=-\\tanh(y)$, so $u^m_t$ is odd. For $y>0$, [F7] gives $\\exp(0)=\\exp(0)^2$, and positivity [F9] therefore gives $\\exp(0)=1$; strict increase [F8] now yields $r:=\\exp(2y)>1$. Put $s:=(r-1)/(r+1)\\in(0,1)$. Rewriting $\\tanh(y)$ and $\\tanh(2y)$ using [F7, F9] gives $$\\tanh(y)=s,\\qquad \\tanh(2y)=\\frac{r^2-1}{r^2+1}=\\frac{2s}{1+s^2}<2s.$$ Thus for every $x>0$, step 2.1 gives $$u^m_t(2x)-2u^m_t(x)=\\beta\\bigl(\\tanh(2kx)-2\\tanh(kx)\\bigr)<0.$$ An odd affine function must be linear through the origin, and every such function has $u(2x)-2u(x)=0$. Hence $u^m_t$ is not affine and is nonlinear.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_smoke": [
        {
          "check": "ml101-flow-two-point-symmetric-mixture",
          "asserts": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and fix $a>0$ and $\\sigma_{\\min}>0$. Let the endpoint law assign probability $1/2$ to each endpoint $x_1=-a$ and $x_1=a$. For each endpoint, use the one-dimensional Gaussian path with\n$$\\sigma_t=1-(1-\\sigma_{\\min})t,\\qquad X_t=\\sigma_t Z+t x_1,\\qquad Z\\sim N(0,1).$$\nFor either endpoint and each fixed $Z=z$, the conditional particle trajectory is a straight line. The marginal density is\n$$p_t(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,\\sigma_t}\\left[ \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{(x-ta)^2}{2\\sigma_t^2}\\right)+ \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{(x+ta)^2}{2\\sigma_t^2}\\right) \\right],$$\nand its marginal velocity field, defined by averaging the conditional velocity against the endpoint density at fixed $(t,x)$, is\n$$u^m_t(x)=\\frac{\\sigma_{\\min}-1}{\\sigma_t}x+ \\frac{a}{\\sigma_t}\\tanh\\!\\left(\\frac{atx}{\\sigma_t^2}\\right), \\qquad \\tanh(y):=\\frac{\\exp(y)-\\exp(-y)}{\\exp(y)+\\exp(-y)}.$$\nThe denominator in the definition of $\\tanh$ is positive for every real input. The density is strictly positive, and $\\partial_tp_t(x)+\\partial_x(p_t(x)u^m_t(x))=0$ on $(0,1)\\times\\mathbb R$. For every $t\\in(0,1)$, $x\\mapsto u^m_t(x)$ is nonlinear.",
          "claim": "Finite symbolic check of the nonlinear marginal field.",
          "checked_in": "Verification steps 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, 3.1.",
