        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ (Continuity of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point of $A$ and on $A$: the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ condition, its agreement with $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)$ at a limit point, and continuity at an isolated point, Intervals of $\\mathbb{R}$: the nine order-convex forms, nondegeneracy, and length) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n(The derivative $f'(c) = \\lim_{x \\to c} \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c}$ of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point $c \\in A$ that is a limit point of $A$, and differentiability on a set). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix $k$ and a history for which $\\theta_k=\\theta\\in\\Theta$ and both losses at $\\theta$ are finite. Choose a compact parameter box $H\\subset\\Theta$ with $\\theta$ in its interior. Changing coordinates one at a time, the mean value theorem [F14] and the derivative majorants in [F2] bound the differences between each loss integrand at $\\theta'$ and at $\\theta$ by the corresponding sums of $|\\theta'_j-\\theta_j|G^C_{H,j}$ and $|\\theta'_j-\\theta_j|G^M_{H,j}$. Integrating these bounds proves local finiteness of both losses throughout the interior of $H$, so [F2] applies there and gives $\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_C(\\theta)=\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_M(\\theta)$. For fixed $w=(t,x_1,x)$, put $a_\\theta:=v(t,x;\\theta)-u^c_t(x\\mid x_1)$. Bilinearity and symmetry [F5] give $$\\|a_{\\theta+\\varepsilon e_j}\\|_2^2-\\|a_\\theta\\|_2^2=\\langle a_{\\theta+\\varepsilon e_j}-a_\\theta,a_{\\theta+\\varepsilon e_j}+a_\\theta\\rangle.$$ Dividing by $\\varepsilon$ and taking the limit using differentiability of $v$ shows that the $j$th coordinate of the sample gradient is $2\\langle\\partial_{\\theta_j}v,v-u^c\\rangle$, exactly the $j$th coordinate of $\\zeta(\\theta,w)$. Since the triple has law $Q$, the expectation change-of-variables formula [F10] identifies its $Q$-mean with $\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_C(\\theta)$. Thus its $Q$-mean is $\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_M(\\theta)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F10",
            "F14",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Each $\\zeta_{k,b}$, and hence $g_k$, is integrable by the explicit batch-integrability assumption. For fixed $\\theta$, put $m(\\theta):=\\int_Q\\zeta(\\theta,w)\\,Q(dw)$. Joint measurability, Tonelli [F13], and the local derivative majorants make $m$ measurable and finite; at each visited parameter step 1.1 gives $m(\\theta_k)=\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_M(\\theta_k)$. Fubini [F11] and the integral triangle inequality [F12] show that each coordinate of $m(\\theta_k)$ is integrable, with its absolute expectation bounded by that of the corresponding sample-gradient coordinate. For every $A\\in\\mathcal F_k$, independence and the product-law fact [F9], the change-of-variables formula [F10], and Fubini [F11] give $$\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_A g_k]=\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\mathbf1_A\\int_{Q^{\\otimes B}}\\frac1B\\sum_{b=1}^B\\zeta(\\theta_k,w_b)\\,Q^{\\otimes B}(d(w_1,\\ldots,w_B))\\right]=\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_A\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_M(\\theta_k)],$$ where step 1.1 identifies each fixed-parameter $Q$-mean and [F6] gives linearity. Thus [F8] makes $\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal L_M(\\theta_k)$ a version of $\\mathbb E[g_k\\mid\\mathcal F_k]$; the batch estimate is unbiased conditionally on its pre-batch history. The arithmetic update is defined for every nonnegative step size when $\\theta_k\\in\\Theta$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F6",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "F13"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For the realized $\\theta_N$ satisfying the exact-flow bounds, [F3] identifies the sampling equation as a parameterized ODE and [F4] gives its unique solution on the full interval and joint continuity of $(x,\\theta)\\mapsto\\Phi^\\theta_{1,0}(x)$. The terminal output is therefore measurable as a function of the independent initial draw and the training history. Conditional on $\\mathcal F_N$, $\\theta_N$ is fixed, so [F7] identifies the conditional output law by $\\mu_0(\\{x:\\Phi^{\\theta_N}_{1,0}(x)\\in A\\})$ for each Borel set $A$. This verifies that the exact-flow output is defined; it makes no claim that the learned field realizes the target path.",
          "step": "3.1",
